---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-vertigo`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Vertigo (BPPV)',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'H81.1', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Vertigo', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Vertigo (BPPV): Can It Cut Recurrences?"
  description="Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo involves calcium crystals in the inner ear. A notable randomised trial found vitamin D plus calcium reduced BPPV recurrence in deficient patients."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-vertigo", label: "Vitamin D & Vertigo" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Vertigo (BPPV)</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      The most common cause of true spinning vertigo is benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV) —
      brief, intense episodes triggered by head movements like rolling over in bed or looking up. It
      is caused by tiny calcium-carbonate crystals (otoconia) that have come loose in the inner ear.
      Because those crystals are made of calcium, and calcium metabolism runs on vitamin D, this is one
      of the few "vitamin D and…" topics with a genuinely encouraging randomised trial behind it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The crystal connection</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The otoconia in the inner ear are calcium-carbonate crystals embedded in a matrix. Their normal
      turnover and reabsorption depend on local calcium regulation, and vitamin D and oestrogen both
      influence that biology — which may partly explain why BPPV is more common in older women and in
      people who are vitamin D deficient. Observational studies have repeatedly found lower 25(OH)D in
      people with BPPV, and especially in those who suffer <em>recurrent</em> episodes.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The trial worth knowing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The standout evidence is a large randomised trial by Jeong and colleagues (Neurology, 2020). In
      patients who had just been successfully treated for BPPV and had low vitamin D, giving
      <strong>vitamin D 400 IU plus calcium 500 mg twice daily</strong> significantly reduced the
      recurrence rate over the following year compared with observation — the annual recurrence dropped
      from about 1.1 to 0.8 episodes per person, roughly a 24% relative reduction, with the benefit
      concentrated in those who were most deficient at baseline. This is a rare example of a
      well-designed vitamin D trial with a positive, clinically meaningful result.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What still comes first</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Vitamin D does not stop an acute attack. The definitive treatment for active BPPV is a
      <strong>canalith repositioning manoeuvre</strong> — most commonly the Epley manoeuvre — which
      physically moves the crystals out of the sensitive semicircular canal and resolves symptoms in
      the great majority of people, often in a single session. Vitamin D and calcium are a
      <em>prevention</em> strategy layered on top, aimed at reducing how often it comes back in people
      who are deficient.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical takeaway</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Get positional vertigo properly diagnosed and treated with a repositioning manoeuvre.</li>
      <li>If you have recurrent BPPV, ask about checking your 25(OH)D.</li>
      <li>If you are deficient, correcting it (with calcium) is a reasonable, evidence-backed way to lower the recurrence rate.</li>
      <li>Vertigo has many other causes — sudden vertigo with new hearing loss, double vision, weakness, or severe headache needs urgent assessment.</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Not all dizziness is BPPV. Vertigo accompanied by
      neurological symptoms, new hearing loss, or a severe headache can signal something serious and
      should be assessed promptly.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-vertigo" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
